      "[Document(metadata={'producer': 'www.ilovepdf.com', 'creator': 'Microsoft® Word 2016', 'creationdate': '2026-09-05T13:52:14+00:00', 'author': 'SIERD training', 'moddate': '2026-09-05T13:52:15+00:00', 'source': 'C:\\\\Users\\\\HP\\\\Downloads\\\\Python\\\\PracticeQuestions\\\\Datasets\\\\IRass.pdf', 'total_pages': 2, 'page': 0, 'page_label': '1'}, page_content='Assignment – Module 1: Information Retrieval \\nTotal Marks: 30 \\nInstructions \\n\\uf0b7 Write your Name and Roll No. on the first page. \\n\\uf0b7 Take clear photographs/scans of all pages and combine them into one PDF .  \\n\\uf0b7 Upload the PDF on Google Classroom.  \\n\\uf0b7 Also submit the handwritten assignment.  \\n\\uf0b7 Use the prescribed book as the primary reference. PPTs are only for understanding.  \\n\\uf0b7 Follow the book’s notations, terminology, and standard conventions.  \\n\\uf0b7 Show all intermediate steps wherever required.  \\n \\nQ1. Consider the following document collection:          6 Marks \\n\\uf0b7 D1: information retrieval uses indexing  \\n\\uf0b7 D2: indexing improves information search  \\n\\uf0b7 D3: information search uses efficient retrieval  \\n(a) Construct the inverted index for the collection. [3] \\n(b) Using your index, process the Boolean query: \\ninformation AND retrieval [2] \\nShow the postings-list operations involved. \\n(c) Explain how the query could be processed more efficiently. [1] \\n \\nQ2. Consider:                         5 Marks \\n\\uf0b7 D1: information retrieval systems process information  \\n\\uf0b7 D2: information systems support retrieval  \\n\\uf0b7 D3: efficient information retrieval  \\n(a) Construct the positional index for information, retrieval, and systems. [3] \\n(b) Demonstrate how the phrase query: \\n\"information retrieval\" \\nis processed using the positional index. [2]'), Document(metadata={'producer': 'www.ilovepdf.com', 'creator': 'Microsoft® Word 2016', 'creationdate': '2026-09-05T13:52:14+00:00', 'author': 'SIERD training', 'moddate': '2026-09-05T13:52:15+00:00', 'source': 'C:\\\\Users\\\\HP\\\\Downloads\\\\Python\\\\PracticeQuestions\\\\Datasets\\\\IRass.pdf', 'total_pages': 2, 'page': 1, 'page_label': '2'}, page_content='Q3. Consider the wildcard query:            6 Marks \\nre*ve \\n(a) Show how this query is processed using a Permuterm index. [2] \\n(b) Using a 3-gram index, add the boundary symbol $ and derive the Boolean k-gram query. \\n[2] \\n(c) Explain why candidate terms obtained from a k-gram index require post-filtering before \\nretrieving their postings. [2] \\n \\nQ4. Suppose the user enters the misspelled term:                     5 Marks \\nretrival \\nand the vocabulary contains: \\nretrieval, removal, arrival, retrievals \\n(a) Explain how k-gram overlap can be used to generate candidate corrections. [2] \\n(b) Calculate the edit distance(Levenshtein) between retrival and retrieval. [2] \\n(c) Explain why candidate generation should be performed before calculating edit distance \\nfor every vocabulary term. [1] \\n \\nQ5. A search engine needs to index a collection that is too large to fit completely in main \\nmemory.               8 Marks \\n(a) Describe how BSBI (Blocked Sort-Based Indexing) can be used to construct the \\ninverted index. Show the major steps. [3] \\n(b) Explain how SPIMI (Single-Pass In-Memory Indexing) would construct the same index \\nand identify one major difference from BSBI. [3] \\n(c) If the collection is continuously updated, explain how dynamic indexing can be used to \\nhandle new documents efficiently. [2]')]\n"
